  "content": {
    "id": "p30-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Prove that the function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ where $f(x)=x^3$ is a bijection.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$f$ is injective and surjective, hence bijective.\n\nEquality of outputs forces equality of inputs; every real output $y$ has the input $\\sqrt[3]{y}$.",
      "worked": "Injectivity. If $f(x)=f(y)$, then\n$$\\begin{align*}\nx^3&=y^3\\\\\nx^3-y^3&=0\\\\\n(x-y)(x^2+xy+y^2)&=0\\\\\n(x-y)\\left(x^2+xy+\\frac{y^2}{4}-\\frac{y^2}{4}+y^2\\right)&=0\\\\\n(x-y)\\left(\\left(x+\\frac y2\\right)^2+\\frac{3y^2}{4}\\right)&=0.\n\\end{align*}$$\nIf the second factor is zero, both squares are zero, giving $x=y=0$. Otherwise, $x-y=0$. Thus $x=y$ in either case, so $f$ is injective.\n\nSurjectivity. For any $y\\in\\mathbb{R}$, choose $x=\\sqrt[3]{y}\\in\\mathbb{R}$. Then\n$$f\\left(\\sqrt[3]{y}\\right)=\\left(\\sqrt[3]{y}\\right)^3=y.$$\nThus $f$ is surjective. Since $f$ is both injective and surjective, it is a bijection.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 85
  },
